\subsection{The 12-column grid}{

Inside \code{row()}, the \code{col()} widths are out of 12. A row whose columns
add up to less than 12 leaves the rest empty, \code{offset} leaves empty
columns before a column, and columns that do not fit on the line wrap
onto the next one, as in Shiny. A \code{row()} of other widgets is a plain
\code{\link[=horizontal]{horizontal()}}. Outside a \code{row()}, a \code{col()} is simply \code{"<width>fr"} wide.

A row is as tall as its content. A terminal does not scroll the page the
way a browser does, so a row holding a long table would run off the
screen: \code{row(..., fill = TRUE)} makes the row take the height left over
by the other rows instead, with its columns as tall as the row, and the
table scrolls inside it.

The layout column is called \code{col()} because \code{column()} is the
\code{\link[=data_table]{data_table()}} column spec. \code{row()} and \code{col()} mask base R's matrix
functions of the same names when termr is attached; given a matrix or
data frame they call \code{\link[base:row]{base::row()}} and \code{\link[base:col]{base::col()}}, so existing code
keeps working.
}

\subsection{Tabs}{

\code{tabsetPanel(id = "tabs")} makes the selected tab readable as
\code{input$tabs}: the \code{value} of the selected \code{tabPanel()}, which defaults to
its title. React to a change with \code{observeEvent(input$tabs, ...)} and
switch tabs with \code{\link[=updateTabsetPanel]{updateTabsetPanel()}}.
}
}
